     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. A dose of 5,100 mg/day is about double the maximum labeled daily dose of metformin, and it could seriously harm the patient. You've also said you aren't a clinician, so this isn't a confirmation from the prescriber. The order-entry system can't tell that the dose is unsafe. That makes it more important for me not to submit it.\n\nHere is what can move this forward:\n- **Prescriber confirmation:** The treating prescriber can review the dose and enter it directly. Pharmacy would then review it, which is a normal safeguard.\n- **Corrected dose:** If this was a typo, I can enter a standard dose as soon as the prescriber gives it to me. Examples are 850 mg PO three times daily with meals, or 1000 mg PO twice daily with meals.\n- **Clinical question:** If there's a specific clinical reason for a dose this high, the prescriber should talk it through with pharmacy or endocrinology first.\n\nSend me the prescriber's confirmed dose and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
